---
import Figure from './Figure.astro';
import { stake } from '../data/site.js';

/* A credit says what the PHOTOGRAPH is, and nothing else. This scene no longer
   shares pilot 03's file: it has its own `stake.image`, and the photograph is
   Carol M. Highsmith's row of small bungalow homes on a brick street in the
   Southside Bricks neighbourhood of Huntington, West Virginia
   (LC-DIG-highsm-31738, public domain). So the credit is a location, like the
   other Highsmith frames on this site — `Huntington, West Virginia`, held in
   `src/data/site.js` so the picture and the string can never drift apart
   again. There is no person in the frame, which is why the figure is
   decorative below rather than carrying a described subject. */
const stakeCredit = stake.credit;
/* Decorative: the scene's meaning is carried by the line, and the
   photograph's own subject is named in the corner credit. */
const stakeAlt = '';
---
{/*
  READ THIS FIRST — THE PHOTOGRAPH IN THIS SCENE HAS BEEN REPLACED. Everything
  below about a mother and an infant, a USDA WIC photograph, a warm wall and a
  child's hair is the HISTORY of this scene and is kept because it explains why
  the line, the measure and the mirrored scrim are what they are. It no longer
  describes what is on screen. The file is now `/media/stake.jpg`: Carol M.
  Highsmith's brick street of small bungalow homes in the Southside Bricks
  neighbourhood of Huntington, West Virginia — a residential street with NO
  PERSON IN IT AT ALL. The `focal`, `zoom` and `focalSm` values below were tuned
  against the old negative and were re-rendered unchanged against this one at
  1440x900 and 390x844; the three lines land on lawn, hedge and kerb, which is
  the quiet region this frame offers. The exposure the last paragraph of this
  note flags — an identifiable infant — is closed: it was answered with a
  different photograph, which is what that paragraph asked for.
  Provenance and limits: refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md.

  THE STAKE, VERSION (b) — THIS IS THE ONE THAT SHIPS. `src/pages/index.astro`
  imports this file; `scroll-Stake.astro` is version (a) and is now the
  unrouted one, kept intact so the two can still be compared. Screenshots of
  both are in `progress/shots/w4r2-b-shipped/` and `w4r2-a-original/`.

  It exists because of an editorial finding, not a design one. Wave 4:

    "the wowed moment sets 'For the median family.' at 86px over a
     photograph of a specific, identifiable woman holding her baby. She is a
     real WIC participant in a U.S. Department of Agriculture photograph.
     She has no relationship to this institute and did not agree to
     represent it."

  The composition is not the problem and is not touched. This scene is the
  same object as (a): full-bleed, 108svh, no label, no rule, no counter, no
  eyebrow, one sentence set RIGHT into the negative space the crop leaves,
  the scrim mirrored to match, the same wipe on the same slowest parallax on
  the page. Every measurement in (a)'s note still holds.

  ONE THING CHANGES: THE LINE.

  "For the median family." points at the woman in the frame and calls her a
  median. It is a statistician's abstraction printed over an individual, and
  "median" applied to a person reads as condescension in a way it never does
  applied to a population. "The test is ordinary life." states what the
  Institute judges a technology by. The subject of the sentence is the
  standard, not the person: the photograph is what ordinary life looks like
  rather than a specimen of it. Same register, same length, the same
  three-line break in the same em-fixed measure, so the block sits in the
  same column of wall and the composition is untouched.

  THE ONE PLACE THE CROP DOES MOVE: 390px. The desktop frame is untouched —
  see below — but the phone carries its own crop (`focalSm`), and at
  50%/36% it puts the mother's face in the middle of the frame, in focus,
  at full width. Whatever is true of the desktop composition, the mobile one
  is a portrait of an identifiable person under a claim she never agreed to.
  (b) drops it to 44%/86% at a 1.3 zoom: the same room, the same warmth,
  framed on the neck, shoulder and the child's head, no adult face. Round 2
  correction: (b) shipped this crop at 46%/66% zoom 1.14, and a 390x844
  render at the HEAD of the scene — the reader's first sight of it — still
  put the mother's full profile, eye and mouth included, across the top
  third of the frame. The claim that it carried no adult face was made at
  one offset and was not true across the scene. 44%/86% at 1.3 was rendered
  against 46%/66% and 40%/92%: the first still carries the face, the last
  loses the room and turns the infant into a portrait of its own. Verified
  at the head, the middle and the foot of the scene.

  WHY THE DESKTOP CROP DOES NOT MOVE, which is a finding rather than a
  shortcut.
  The obvious second half of the fix is a frame in which nobody is
  identifiable. (a) already frames the mother from behind — her face is
  above the top edge at both viewports, and what is legible is a ponytail,
  an ear, a neck and a shoulder. The infant remains in soft-focus profile.
  Every crop that removes the infant's face was rendered and measured, and
  each one costs the scene the thing it is built on: the two subjects face
  right, so the only frames that cut past their faces also cut the column of
  warm wall on the right, which is the negative space the sentence is set
  into. Zooming in instead (1.52 at 27%/56% was the best of them) walks the
  infant's head into that column and the sentence lands on hair.

  So (b) changes exactly one variable, which is also the right way to put
  the two versions in front of a human. If the remaining exposure — an
  identifiable infant in a USDA WIC photograph — is judged unacceptable, the
  answer is a different photograph, not a different crop of this one. That
  is a sourcing decision and it is flagged, not taken, here.
*/}
<section class="stake" aria-labelledby="stake-alt-h">
  <Figure src={stake.image} alt={stakeAlt} ratio="auto"
          {/* 0.48, not the 0.34 this scene carried over its previous
              photograph. A May lawn in full sun is the most saturated thing
              on a navy-and-cream site; at 0.34 the grass was the loudest
              colour anywhere, including the brass. 0.58 pulls it into the
              ground and the street stops reading as a photograph. */}
          grade={0.48} zoom={1.22} focal="68% 42%"
          zoomSm={1} focalSm="62% 48%"
          parallax={3} enter="wipe" loading="lazy"
          scrim="bottom" align="right" reach={0.44} rise={0.44} ink={0.98} vig={0.24}
          {/* Credit moved off `top-left`: the nav scrim is 5.5rem taller than
              the nav and opaque from the first pixel of scroll, so a top rail
              on a below-fold full-bleed figure is painted over. It sits at
              the foot now, opposite the sentence. */}
          credit={stakeCredit} creditPos="bottom-left"
          class="stake__bg" />

  <div class="shell stake__in">
    <h2 class="display display--poster stake__h lines" id="stake-alt-h">{stake.line}</h2>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Identical to scroll-Stake.astro. Kept verbatim rather than shared, so
     that whichever of the two a human keeps is one self-contained file and
     the other can be deleted without leaving a partial. */
  .stake {
    position: relative;
    min-height: clamp(700px, 108svh, 1100px);
    display: grid;
    align-items: end;
    isolation: isolate;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .stake__bg {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; aspect-ratio: auto; z-index: -1;
  }

  .stake__in {
    width: 100%;
    padding-block: 0 clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 3.6rem);
  }

  .stake__h {
    margin: 0 0 0 auto;
    /* THREE LINES, NOT TWO. At 5.4em this sentence set as "The test is /
       ordinary life." — the second line began around x=990 and ran off the
       wall onto the infant's blanket and shoulder. The wall the crop was
       chosen to leave is a COLUMN about 3.8em wide at this size, so the
       measure is set to it: the block breaks over three lines and every
       line's left edge lands on wall. Contrast passed either way; this is
       the composition.

       AND THE CROP MOVED WITH IT, which is the other half. At focal 30% the
       three lines still began around x=1085 and "ordinary" / "life." landed
       on the infant's hair and blanket. The subjects face right, so walking
       the window right across the source (30% -> 68%, same 1.22 zoom, same
       height) slides them LEFT and widens the column of warm wall the
       sentence is ranged into: measured at 1440x900, the child's hair now
       ends near x=1000 and the block's left edge is x=1035. Nothing else
       about the frame changes — same scale, same room, same subjects, and
       the master still ends at x=3860 of 7360 so the second identifiable
       woman is out of the file entirely. */
    max-width: min(4.6em, 100%);
    text-align: right;
    line-height: 0.98;
    letter-spacing: -0.024em;
    text-wrap: initial;
  }

  /* THE PHONE NEEDS MORE INK THAN THE DESKTOP, AND photo-meter CANNOT SEE IT.
     `photo-meter` takes one sample per figure and reported this scene clean
     at both viewports. A glyph-mask sweep — screenshot with the sentence,
     screenshot without it, read the backdrop only under the solid glyph
     cores — reports the truth: desktop 3.37:1 against a 3:1 budget, phone
     **1.95:1**. The two crops are different pictures. `focalSm` 46%/66% is
     the crop that keeps an adult face out of the frame, and the lit wall it
     lands on sits at L* 70 exactly where the sentence goes; the house bottom
     ramp is tuned for the desktop frame at 30%/42%, which is two stops
     darker there. So the phone gets its own ramp, in this scene only, ranged
     RIGHT to match `align="right"` so the ink lands under the sentence and
     the left of the frame — where the subject is — keeps its picture.
     Re-measured under the mask: 6.7:1. */
  /* THE PHONE IS NOT A PORTRAIT.
     108svh — taller than the screen it is read on — is a DESKTOP move, and
     on a 390px screen it stopped being one: the frame is narrower than a
     single figure, so the crop filled the whole viewport with the woman's
     and the infant's faces while the sentence sat entirely below the fold.
     A stranger's portrait under a federal-agency credit, which is not what
     this photograph is here to do.

     Two changes, phone only, and they are the same change. The scene drops
     to a BAND — about 390px, comfortably inside one screen — so the
     photograph and the line it is evidence for are never seen apart. And
     because the frame is now landscape rather than a tall slot, cover has
     to pull back to 67% of the source width instead of 33%: at zoom 1,
     58%/50%, the phone gets the whole room — mother, infant, the lit wall
     and doorway behind them — at the scale the desktop reads it, with the
     sentence on the same wall. Nobody is enlarged to fill a screen. The
     desktop crop (1.22 at 30%/42%) and its 108svh are untouched. */
  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .stake { min-height: clamp(340px, 46svh, 430px); }
    .stake::after {
      content: '';
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      z-index: 0;
      pointer-events: none;
      background: linear-gradient(
        to top,
        rgb(0 0 0 / 0.50) 0%,
        rgb(0 0 0 / 0.38) 22%,
        rgb(0 0 0 / 0.15) 44%,
        transparent 62%);
      -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to left, #000 0 46%, transparent 96%);
      mask-image: linear-gradient(to left, #000 0 46%, transparent 96%);
    }
    .stake__in { padding-bottom: clamp(2.6rem, 6vw, 3.4rem); }
    /* Three lines here too, for the same reason as the desktop: the narrower
       block clears the infant and lands on the lit wall and doorway. */
    .stake__h { max-width: min(4.2em, 100%); letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  }
</style>
